And when he had gone forth to the sea side, all the multitude pressed upon him to hear the word of God. And he saw two ships standing by the lake: but the fishermen were gone out of them, and were washing their nets. And he entered into one of the ships, which was Simon's, and prayed him that he would thrust out a little from the land. And he sat down, and taught the people out of the ship.
Jesus said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
They closed their windows and followed him.
And when he had increased, he saw James and John, sons of Zebedee, in their boat mending their nets.
And when they came to him, he commanded the unclean spirit to come out of them; and it came out without doing them harm. Jesus cast out demons (Luke 4:31-37)
After that Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. On the Sabbath day, Jesus entered the synagogue and began teaching.
The attendees were amazed at his teaching style because he taught them like someone in authority, unlike how Jewish books teach.
There was a man in the complex who had lost his soul
He said: "What do you want with me, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us?" I know who you are - the Holy One of God!"
But Jesus rebuked him, saying, "Be silent, and come out of him!"
The demon possessed his soul, he screamed loudly and got thrown out.
Everyone was amazed and started asking each other: "What is this? A new teaching with authority! Even demons obey him when he commands them!"
And soon he spread the news about Jesus throughout all the regions of Galilee. Simon's mother-in-law was healed (Matthew 8:14-17; Luke 4:38-41)
As soon as Jesus and his disciples left the temple, they went to Simon Peter's house with James and John.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed with fever, and they told Jesus about her.
He came up to her and took hold of her hand and helped her up; then he made her serve them all.
في المسا لما الشمس غربت، الناس جتله بالمرضى اللي كانوا عايشين في المنطقة كلها
And all the people in Jerusalem gathered together before the temple.
He healed many who were sick with various diseases, and he cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak because they knew who he was. Jesus went away to Galilee (Luke 4:42-44)
And early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up, left the house, and went off to a secluded place, and was praying there.
Simon left with his companion to visit him
And when he was found, they said to him, "Everyone is looking for you!"
He said to them, "Let's go to the other towns nearby so that I can preach there too, because that is why I came."
He went around preaching in synagogues throughout Galilee and expelling demons. A man suffering from leprosy came to him and begged for help, saying, "Lord, if you are willing, you have power to make me clean." Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing," he said. "Be clean!" And immediately the leprosy left the man. Then Jesus warned him not to tell anyone about what happened. Instead, he ordered him to go to the priest and offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded for his cleansing, as a testimony to everyone. (Matthew 8:1-4; Luke 5:12-16)
And there was an ugly man who came up to him and said: "If you want me to be cured, I'll do it."
My friend, Jesus had mercy on him, stretched his hand and said: "I love you, be healed!"
As soon as Jesus spoke, leprosy left him and he was healed.
And when it was time for Jesus to leave, he called him over
# 13:49; 14:2-32 They said to him, "Look, don't tell anyone about this. But go to the priest and have him offer sacrifice for your cleansing, just as Moses commanded in the Law. This way you will be cleansed."
But he went away and began to proclaim it loudly, spreading the news until even Jesus could no longer enter a town openly but had to stay in deserted places, and people were coming to him from every quarter.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man (Matthew 9:1-8; Luke 5:17-26)
After several days Jesus returned to Capernaum, and people heard about it and came to his house.
He gathered many people until there was no more space even for someone standing at the door. Then he started talking about Allah's word.
They brought him a paralyzed man who was being carried by four men.
But they couldn't get close because of the crowd, so they went up onto the roof and lowered him through the tiles right in front of Jesus.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And among those people there were some Jewish scribes who began to think in their hearts:
Does this man think he can talk like that? He's committing blasphemy! Who else but God alone has the power to forgive sins?
And in time Jesus knew what they were thinking about and asked them: "Why do you think this way?"
Is it easier to say to someone who is paralyzed: "Your sins have been forgiven," or to tell them: "Get up, shake off your mat, and walk"?
But that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins—he said to the man who was paralyzed,
"Get up, shake off your bedclothes, and go home."
He got up early in the morning, shook off his bedclothes, and went ahead of everyone else. They all followed him, amazed at what they saw, praising God and saying, "We've never seen anything like this before!" Jesus calls Matthew (Matthew 9:9-13; Luke 5:27-32)
Jesus went back to the lakeshore again and all the people came crowding around him to listen to his teaching.
And when he was walking by, Levi son of Alphaeus, who was sitting at the tax booth, saw him; and he said to him, "Follow me." So he arose and followed Him.
While Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house, many tax collectors and sinners came and ate with him and his disciples, for there were many who followed him.
And when the Pharisees saw that Jesus was eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this and answered them, "Healthy people don't need a doctor; sick people do. I haven't come to call righteous people but sinners." The discussion about fasting (Matthew 9:14-17; Luke 5:33-39)
And John's disciples were fasting, along with the Pharisees. Then they came to Jesus and asked, "Why do John's disciples fast but yours don't?"
Tell them: "How can you expect guests to fast when the groom is still among them? As long as he's with them, they cannot fast."
Soon it will be time for them to fast while their groom is being taken away from them.
There wasn't even one person who could patch an old garment with new fabric without the new piece shrinking and eating away at the old garment, making it worse in the end.
And there wasn't even one person who would put new wine in old wineskins so that the new wine wouldn't burst the skins. "No, new wine must be put into new wineskins." The conversation took place on Saturday (Mark 2:23-28; Luke 6:1-5)
And Jesus was passing through the grain fields on a Sabbath day, and his disciples began to pluck heads of grain while walking along.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Why do your disciples break the Sabbath?"
Tell them: "Bro, haven't you read what happened to David and his men when they were hungry? They needed food."
# 1 Sam 21:1-6; 2 Sam 8:17 How did he enter God's house during Abiathar's time when he was the highest priest? And how could he eat from the holy bread that priests were allowed to eat alone, and even give some to his friends?»
And he said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
I am the son of man, Lord of the Sabbath."
The man with a withered hand was healed (Mark 3:1-5; Luke 6:6-11)
Jesus went back to the temple again, where there was a man with a withered hand.
They started watching him to see if he would heal that man by Saturday so they could accuse him.
Hey man! He told the guy with dry hands: "Get up and stand in the middle!"
And then he said to them: "Hey guys! Is it okay for us to do good or bad things on Saturday? Should we kill someone?" But they just kept quiet.
Jesus was watching them while he felt sad because their hearts were hard. He said to the man, "Stretch your hand!" The man did so, and his hand became healthy again.
The Pharisees left the assembly and started to plot with Herod's party members how they could kill him.
Jesus and his disciples went to the other side of the lake, followed by many people from Galilee and Judea.
And Jerusalem and Edomites and people from all directions beyond the Jordan River and many others from places around Tyre and Sidon heard about what happened.
Jesus told his disciples to get him a boat so that he could sit in it while teaching the crowds and wouldn't be crowded by them.
He healed many people for his sake, my friend. Anyone who's sick can come to him to be cured.
And the disembodied spirits would come to worship him while calling out, "You are God's son!"
And he ordered them strictly not to make him known. The twelve apostles (Matthew 10:1-4; Luke 6:12-16)
And then Jesus went up on a mountain and called to those he wanted with him. And they came to him.
He chose twelve men to be with him and he sent them out two by two with power over evil spirits.
They have the power to remove the judges.
The twelve he chose were Simon whom he named Peter
James and John, sons of Zebedee, whom he named Boanerges, which means "sons of thunder"
Andraos, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, Jacob son of Halphi, Thaddeus, Simon the Zealot
Judas Iscariot who will betray him. Jesus and Beelzebul (Matthew 12:22-32; Luke 11:14-23; 12:10)
Jesus went to a house where people had gathered again in such large numbers that there was no room left, even outside near the door, and he couldn't eat his meal.
When his relatives heard that he was being taken to jail, they said, "He's lost his mind."
And this is what the Jews who had come down from Jerusalem were saying, "He's driven out by Beelzebul, the prince of demons, and he drives out demons with Beelzebul, their prince!"
Jesus called them to him and said in parables: "How can Satan drive out Satan?
If a kingdom is divided against itself, it cannot stand.
If the house is divided among several people, that house cannot be stable.
